---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import GasLawsSim from '../../simulators/gaslaws/GasLawsSim.astro';
import { gasLawsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/gaslaws';

const toc = [
  { id: 'pressure', label: 'What is gas pressure?' },
  { id: 'kelvin', label: 'Temperature in kelvin' },
  { id: 'boyle', label: 'Boyle’s law' },
  { id: 'charles', label: 'Charles’s law' },
  { id: 'pt', label: 'The pressure–temperature law' },
  { id: 'ideal', label: 'The ideal gas law' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms and formulas' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 9.2: Relating Pressure, Volume, Amount, and Temperature: The Ideal Gas Law. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/9-2-relating-pressure-volume-amount-and-temperature-the-ideal-gas-law' },
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 9.5: The Kinetic-Molecular Theory. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/9-5-the-kinetic-molecular-theory' },
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 9.6: Non-Ideal Gas Behavior. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/9-6-non-ideal-gas-behavior' },
  { text: 'NIST (2019). CODATA value: molar gas constant R = 8.314 462 618 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.', url: 'https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?r' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="chemistry"
  topic="gas-laws"
  description="Interactive gas laws simulator: change the volume, temperature and amount of gas in a sealed cylinder, measure pressure from particle collisions, and discover Boyle’s, Charles’s and the pressure–temperature law."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={gasLawsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Why does a bicycle pump get harder to push, a balloon shrink in the freezer, and a car tyre gain pressure on a hot
      day? A gas is billions of particles racing around and crashing into the walls. Squeeze it, heat it or pump more in,
      measure the pressure from the collisions, and uncover the simple laws that every gas follows.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <GasLawsSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong>. Particles race around the cylinder; each flash on a wall is a collision. The <strong>gauge</strong> adds up their pushes to measure the pressure.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Explore</strong>, change the <strong>volume</strong>, <strong>temperature</strong> or <strong>amount of gas</strong> and watch the gauge and the graph.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Boyle</strong> (pressure and volume), <strong>Charles</strong> (volume and temperature) or <strong>P–T</strong> (pressure and temperature). Sliders for the quantities held constant are locked and marked.</li>
      <li>Change the free variable, wait a few seconds for the gauge to settle, and press <strong>Record point</strong>. Collect several points and compare them with the dashed prediction on the graph.</li>
      <li>Click the gauge, thermometer, piston, heater or particles for explanations. <strong>Reset</strong> restores the starting conditions.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="pressure">What is gas pressure?</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>kinetic theory of gases</strong> pictures a gas as tiny particles moving quickly in straight lines,
    bouncing off each other and the walls. Every time a particle bounces off a wall, it gives the wall a small push.
    <strong>Pressure</strong> is the total force of all those pushes on each square metre of wall:
  </p>
  <code class="formula">pressure = force ÷ area   (1 Pa = 1 N/m²)</code>
  <p>
    Pressure rises if the particles hit the walls <strong>more often</strong> or <strong>harder</strong>. Every gas law
    comes from those two ideas. Normal air pressure at sea level is about 101 kPa.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kelvin">Temperature in kelvin</h2>
  <p>
    Temperature measures the average kinetic energy of the particles, and the gas laws only work if it is measured from
    <strong>absolute zero</strong>, the temperature at which particle motion would be at its minimum. The kelvin scale does
    this: 0 K = −273.15 °C, and a change of 1 K equals a change of 1 °C.
  </p>
  <code class="formula">T (K) = T (°C) + 273.15</code>

  <h2 id="boyle">Boyle’s law</h2>
  <p>
    At constant temperature and amount of gas, <strong>pressure is inversely proportional to volume</strong>. Halve the
    volume and the particles have half as far to travel between collisions, so each part of the wall is hit twice as
    often: the pressure doubles. A pressure–volume graph is a curve, and P × V stays the same.
  </p>
  <code class="formula">P₁V₁ = P₂V₂   (T and n constant)</code>

  <h2 id="charles">Charles’s law</h2>
  <p>
    At constant pressure and amount of gas, <strong>volume is proportional to the kelvin temperature</strong>. Heating
    makes the particles faster, so they push harder on a free piston; it rises until the pressure inside again matches the
    pressure outside. Plotted against kelvin temperature, the volume is a straight line through the origin.
  </p>
  <code class="formula">V₁ ÷ T₁ = V₂ ÷ T₂   (P and n constant)</code>

  <h2 id="pt">The pressure–temperature law</h2>
  <p>
    At constant volume and amount of gas, <strong>pressure is proportional to the kelvin temperature</strong>. This is
    often called Gay-Lussac’s law (or Amontons’s law). In a rigid container, faster particles hit the walls both more
    often and harder. Extending the straight line on the graph backwards to zero pressure points to absolute zero, which
    is how scientists first estimated it.
  </p>
  <code class="formula">P₁ ÷ T₁ = P₂ ÷ T₂   (V and n constant)</code>

  <h2 id="ideal">The ideal gas law</h2>
  <p>
    Adding more gas also raises the pressure (or the volume): more particles mean more collisions. This is
    <strong>Avogadro’s law</strong>: at the same temperature and pressure, equal volumes of gas contain equal numbers of
    particles. All four relationships combine into one equation:
  </p>
  <code class="formula">PV = nRT</code>
  <p>
    P is pressure in pascals, V is volume in m³, n is the amount in moles, T is temperature in kelvin and R is the gas
    constant, 8.314 J/(mol·K). For example, 0.20 mol at 300 K in 2.5 L gives P = 0.20 × 8.314 × 300 ÷ 0.0025 ≈ 200 kPa,
    which is the simulator’s starting state.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms and formulas</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Pressure (P)</dt>
    <dd>Force per unit area, measured in pascals (Pa) or kilopascals (kPa).</dd>
    <dt>Absolute zero</dt>
    <dd>0 K, −273.15 °C: the lowest possible temperature.</dd>
    <dt>Ideal gas</dt>
    <dd>A model gas whose particles take up no space and do not attract each other. Real gases behave almost ideally at ordinary temperatures and pressures.</dd>
    <dt>Combined gas law</dt>
    <dd>P₁V₁ ÷ T₁ = P₂V₂ ÷ T₂ for a fixed amount of gas.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Breathing:</strong> your diaphragm enlarges your chest, lowering the air pressure in your lungs so that air flows in (Boyle’s law).</li>
    <li><strong>Hot-air balloons:</strong> heated air expands, so the air inside is less dense than the cooler air outside (Charles’s law).</li>
    <li><strong>Tyres</strong> are checked when cold, because driving warms them and raises their pressure.</li>
    <li><strong>Aerosol cans</strong> carry warnings not to heat them: in a rigid can the pressure rises with temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Divers</strong> must breathe out while rising, because the air in their lungs expands as the water pressure falls.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Heated gas particles get bigger.”</strong> They move faster; the gas takes up more space only because they push the walls further apart.</li>
    <li><strong>“Doubling the Celsius temperature doubles the pressure.”</strong> Only kelvin temperatures are proportional.</li>
    <li><strong>“Pressure comes from the gas’s weight.”</strong> It comes from collisions, and pushes equally in every direction.</li>
    <li><strong>“Gas particles stop at 0 °C.”</strong> At 0 °C (273 K), air molecules average about 450 m/s.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The simulator is two-dimensional and uses an <strong>ideal gas</strong>: each dot represents about 6 × 10²⁰ molecules
    (0.001 mol), the dots take up no space, do not attract each other and, as in the simplest kinetic theory, bounce only
    off the walls and not off each other. The bottom and side walls exchange heat with the gas; the piston is insulated.
    The gauge really is measured from the simulated collisions, averaged over three seconds, and then converted to
    kilopascals with a fixed calibration factor so that it can be compared with PV = nRT. Because there are only a few
    hundred dots, measured values fluctuate by a few percent, just as real measurements have random error. The
    molecular speed readout is the real average speed of nitrogen molecules at the gas temperature; the dots on screen
    move far more slowly so that you can follow them. Real gases deviate from PV = nRT at high pressures, when the
    particles’ own size matters, and at low temperatures, when their attractions matter and they eventually condense.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Gas pressure is caused by particles colliding with the walls of their container.</li>
    <li>Boyle’s law: at constant T and n, P × V is constant.</li>
    <li>Charles’s law: at constant P and n, V ÷ T is constant (T in kelvin).</li>
    <li>Pressure–temperature law: at constant V and n, P ÷ T is constant (T in kelvin).</li>
    <li>All of them combine into the ideal gas law, PV = nRT.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
